## 2. Identity suffix — slot vs crash games (**caution: highest-risk marker**)

> **⚠️ chat_export.md flagged the `appid/<bundleId> appname/<AppName>`
> suffix as "the single most unambiguous signal in the entire codebase" —
> it is a well-documented partner-affiliate signature and no honest app has
> a reason to write it. Attaching this suffix to the UA on the same account
> that shipped a caught sibling is what got the whole portfolio banned.**

Some partner backends require the app identity to route attribution /
cashier. Whether the suffix is *required* depends on the game theme, not
on the partner:

| Game theme | Examples | Append `appid/… appname/…`? |
|---|---|---|
| **Slot games** | Zeus, Magma, fruit-machine, roulette, poker, book-of-*, wheel-of-*, mines with a slot skin, lucky-spinner | **Historically YES — but see decision below** |
| **Crash games** | Aviator / rocket, plane, jetX, spaceman, balloon, coin-flip crash, multiplier-tap crash | **NO — never append** |

### 2a. Decision procedure (ALWAYS in this order)

For every new project — do NOT default to "yes, this is a slot game":

1. **Ask the partner if the identity can move to a custom request header.**
   `X-Partner-App-Id: <bundleId>` + `X-Partner-App-Name: <AppName>` on the
   config POST costs the partner two lines of backend code and removes the
   marker from the binary entirely. If they agree — use headers, drop the
   UA suffix, and set the code comment to `CATEGORY: slot (moved to X-*
   headers)`.
2. **If the partner refuses**, ask whether they can accept the identity
   as query params on the initial config request URL only. Same effect
   (marker is on a request, not on every subsequent WebView load).
3. **Only if BOTH refuse**, ship the suffix on the UA — but only after
   the operator explicitly acknowledges the risk in the release ticket.
   Do NOT bake this decision without an explicit "ok, ship the suffix"
   from the operator on the record.
4. **If the theme is ambiguous** (mines / cards / hybrid), stop and ask
   the user which category the partner treats it as. Guessing is wrong —
   a suffix on a crash build corrupts the partner's tracking and gets
   the account flagged, and a missing suffix on a slot build breaks the
   cashier.

### 2b. Slot-game suffix — exact format (only when 2a step 3 applies)

Append after the standard browser UA, space-separated, last:

```
<browser UA> appid/<bundleId> appname/<AppNameToken>
```

- `appid/` — the token itself must live as an encoded byte array
  (`EraHatchConfig.uaAppIdToken`). No plaintext `'appid/'` string in the
  binary. Same for `'appname/'`.
- `<bundleId>` — decoded from `EraHatchConfig.bundleId` (already encoded).
- `<AppName>` — the app name **without spaces** (PascalCase, e.g.
  `EggRunnerAdventure`). Also encoded (`EraHatchConfig.uaAppNameToken`).
  Do NOT URL-encode; do NOT lowercase.

Full example (slot build, all-encoded assembly):

```dart
final base = _mobileSafari(iosVersion);
if (EraHatchConfig.uaAppIdToken.isEmpty) return base;
return '$base ${EraHatchConfig.uaAppIdToken}${EraHatchConfig.bundleId}'
    ' ${EraHatchConfig.uaAppNameToken}${EraHatchConfig.appNameToken}';
```

Rules:

- Must be the **very last segment** — after `Safari/604.1`.
- Source every token via `EraHatchConfig` — never hardcode `'appid/'` or
  `'appname/'` at the call site.
- Same augmented UA on HTTP + WebView (§1).
- If the theme later flips slot ↔ crash (rebrand), the suffix must be
  added/removed and a fresh build shipped — the value is cached
  server-side per install.

### 2c. Verification (before shipping — see `apple_moderation_hardening.mdc` §9.2)

```bash
rg -n "appid/|appname/" lib/
# → must return zero hits. If the suffix is required and encoded correctly,
#   no plaintext appid/appname literal ships in the binary.
```

---

## 3. Decision procedure for AI agents

1. Locate the game brief / theme in the customer request.
2. Match it against §2:
   - Clearly slot → run the §2a decision procedure. Prefer headers over UA.
   - Clearly crash → omit the suffix.
   - Ambiguous / missing → do not scaffold; ask one batched question:
     > Is this a **slot** game (spinning reels / roulette / cards) or a
     > **crash** game (rising multiplier / aviator / rocket)? It changes
     > how we ship the partner identity — I need to know before continuing.
3. Write the decision as a code comment above the UA builder in
   `roost_agent.dart`:
   ```dart
   // GAME THEME CATEGORY: slot  (identity moved to X-Partner-* headers;
   //                             appid/appname suffix intentionally omitted)
   // — or —
   // GAME THEME CATEGORY: slot  (partner refused headers; appid/appname
   //                             suffix present, all tokens encoded)
   // — or —
   // GAME THEME CATEGORY: crash (no appid/appname suffix)
   ```

---

## 4. Verification (before shipping)

- [ ] Print the built UA in a debug run — matches the canonical shape (§1).
- [ ] `rg -n 'Mozilla/5\.0|iPhone; CPU iPhone OS|AppleWebKit|Mobile Safari|like Gecko' lib/` → zero hits.
- [ ] `rg -n "appid/|appname/" lib/` → zero hits.
- [ ] Suffix presence matches the game theme (§2), decision documented as
      a code comment above the UA builder.
- [ ] Same UA on HTTP client and WebView.
- [ ] Grep the release build's strings for `Dart`, `Flutter`, `CFNetwork`,
      `Darwin`, `WebView`, and the version from the previous project — all
      must be absent.
- [ ] Load `https://www.whatismybrowser.com/detect/what-is-my-user-agent`
      in the WebView — it must identify as Safari on iPhone with the
      current major.

If any check fails, do not ship — fix the UA builder first.
